#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""N.H Claude+Codex design-loop controller.

Scope: DESIGN-ONLY. This controller inspects the local environment and the
configured N.H governance checkout. Exactly one command may ever add anything to
that checkout, execute-next-claude-task, and the single thing it may add is one
new design candidate file under 05_ACTIVE_CANDIDATE/. Every other command writes
nothing but stdout/stderr.

status, preflight and snapshot make no model calls at all. codex-smoke makes
exactly one bounded, read-only Codex model request, claude-smoke makes exactly
one bounded, zero-tool Claude request, claude-read-smoke makes exactly one
bounded Claude request holding read-only tools, next-package makes exactly one
bounded, read-only Codex source review of the N.H checkout, and
prepare-next-claude-task makes at most two such reviews. None of those writes
anything anywhere. execute-next-claude-task runs that same preparation and then,
only if it passes, makes exactly one bounded Claude design run inside a
disposable copy of the source, never inside the real checkout.

next-package asks Ness nothing and never invokes Claude. When the next package
turns out to be a genuine Ness decision it only records that a later
question-validation stage is required; it never states the question.

prepare-next-claude-task reuses that same next-package review and, only when it
returns prepare_claude_task, runs one further read-only Codex review. That
review is the DESIGN-SPECIFICATION stage: GPT confirms the package from source,
reads the governing and direct dependency material, uses the settled Ness
decisions, and DETERMINES THE EXACT MECHANICAL DESIGN, returning it as a strict
mechanical_design_specification. Claude is not the designer here and is never
asked to derive the solution for itself.

The instruction Claude will later receive is CONSTRUCTED BY THIS CONTROLLER:
every operative word is fixed controller-owned text, filled in from four
validated bindings (package ID or its null state, classification, target path,
and that validated specification) plus the settled Ness decision binding the
controller derives itself. No unrestricted model prose reaches it -- not even
the package title, which stays in the report as cross-stage review evidence and
is never made operative. The specification is the one model-authored thing that
becomes operative, and it is operative for DESIGN CONTENT alone: it cannot alter
the target path, the tool set, the file access, the authority order, the
correction cap, the Git prohibition, or authorise any implementation, acceptance
or adoption, and the instruction states each of those limits immediately above
it. When that review cannot state the design because it finds a possible
consequential Ness choice, it returns no specification and no task: the finding
is preserved as a ROUTING SIGNAL for independent question-validation, Claude is
not invoked, and Ness is asked nothing. prepare-next-claude-task too asks Ness
nothing and never invokes Claude.

Claude is required to STOP rather than solve: if the supplied specification is
ambiguous, contradicts source, is incomplete, or would need another consequential
design choice, it outputs the exact marker NH_CLAUDE_SPECIFICATION_STOP and
changes nothing. That marker is a DECLARATION and never the evidence -- the
disposable-workspace proof decides whether a stop actually happened, so a marker
beside a created or changed file is refused as the verification failure it is. A
proved stop creates and promotes nothing, is never called PASS, is never worked
around by asking Claude to invent something, and is classified by one fresh
read-only Codex review as a specification problem, a possible Ness choice, or a
source/technical unknown. Only the second of those records a routing signal, and
none of them asks Ness anything or adds a Claude retry.

execute-next-claude-task is the one command that can add a file to N.H. It runs
the preparation above in memory, and only a fully validated, task_ready,
controller-constructed task unlocks an INITIAL design write. (A resume of an
already authenticated preserved candidate chain creates no initial candidate, so
it is not held to task_ready, to an operative instruction or to a new target; it
passes every common fence and goes straight to a fresh audit of the existing
chain tail.) For an initial write Claude is then given a DISPOSABLE
COPY of the exact committed source, outside N.H, as its working directory: the
real checkout is never Claude's working directory, is never added to its
workspace, and stays read-only to it. Claude may create exactly one file there.
The controller compares that disposable copy against a frozen baseline
manifest, and only if nothing else moved, and the real checkout has not moved
either, does the CONTROLLER itself create that one exact candidate file in the
real repository, with an exclusive no-overwrite create. It never edits an
existing file, never runs git add, commit or push, and never accepts or adopts
anything.

Only after that candidate exists and every promotion and byte-identity check has
passed does execute-next-claude-task run exactly ONE independent, read-only,
ephemeral Codex design audit of that exact real candidate, bound to its verified
path, sha256 and byte length, and returns a validated PASS or BLOCKED. The audit
prompt is entirely controller-owned text over controller-verified facts: no
Claude output reaches it, and it is told that Claude's summary is not evidence.
The complete audit state is proved before the audit, after it, and again
immediately before its result is accepted; a result that cannot be proved is
discarded rather than interpreted.

A validated BLOCKED verdict whose blocking findings are ALL routed
claude_mechanical then opens exactly one bounded automatic correction round:
the controller derives the next versioned candidate path itself, gives every
selected blocker its own controller-derived finding identity, and runs ONE fresh
read-only Codex CORRECTION-SPECIFICATION review that reads the actual blocked
candidate and determines the exact mechanical correction for every one of them.
Claude never works corrections out from audit findings: the findings stay the
auditor's evidence, and the correction is GPT's determination, which every
selected blocker must appear in exactly once by its exact controller-derived
ref. A correction that cannot be stated without a possible Ness choice invokes
no Claude, consumes no blocker, and is preserved as a routing signal instead;
one that cannot be stated for a technical or source reason fails closed. Only
then does the controller freeze a correction binding of controller-validated
facts plus those findings as DATA and that validated specification,
rebuild a disposable workspace holding the frozen source and every
preserved candidate, run Claude once inside it, prove the result is exactly
one new file at exactly that prepared path with every earlier version still
byte-identical, creates it in N.H with the same exclusive no-overwrite
machinery, and runs one FRESH independent Codex audit of the NEW candidate. That
repeats only while another fully mechanical BLOCKED verdict comes back, and at
most MAX_AUTOMATIC_CORRECTION_ROUNDS times. Every earlier candidate is preserved
untouched: nothing is ever overwritten, renamed, deleted, reverted or repaired,
and no version is ever silently skipped. Every candidate path the loop can
create, initial or corrected, is in ONE controlled filename grammar,
<name>_v<major>_<minor>_CANDIDATE.md, which is exactly the set of names the
controller can later version forward by incrementing <minor> alone.

When the command ends it takes one FINAL observation of the real checkout and
re-proves the complete candidate chain against it: the expected HEAD, no staged
or tracked change, exactly those candidates as the whole untracked set, and
every one of them still byte-identical. Every preservation claim in the report
is made from that terminal evidence and from nothing earlier, so a candidate
changed after its own round can never be reported as preserved, and a validated
PASS whose final state can no longer be proved is refused rather than emitted.

The loop stops immediately at a validated PASS, at any Codex technical failure
(never retried), at any BLOCKING finding routed chatgpt_review_required, at the
correction-round cap, at a corrected candidate byte-identical to its
predecessor, and at any handoff, verification, promotion or state failure. A
chatgpt_review_required finding is REPORTED as requiring independent ChatGPT
review; it is never turned into a question for Ness, and this controller
manufactures no Ness decision of any kind. A final PASS means only that the
automatic Claude/Codex mechanical correction loop reached a Codex-PASS
candidate: the independent ChatGPT audit of the actual file, Ness's acceptance
and adoption, package completion, Master and Map integration and any
implementation all remain separate stages that never happen here.

The interview commands are the ONLY path on which a question can ever reach
Ness, and they are deliberately the hardest path here to get onto. A model route
-- genuinely_open_for_ness from next-package, chatgpt_review_required from a
design audit -- is a ROUTING SIGNAL and nothing more: it stops ordinary
mechanical handling, and route-audit-finding records it explicitly as NOT a
question. The same is true of every signal the fresh GPT reviews of this repair raise --
question_coverage_review, design_specification_review,
correction_specification_review and claude_specification_stop_review all route
chatgpt_review_required, all normalise into the SAME pending-routing state a
routed audit finding does, and none of them is ever surfaced to Ness. Only
question-validation, one bounded read-only Codex source check
over the real Master, Map, Defaults, cursorrules, accepted packages, closure and
working records and earlier Ness decisions, can turn an issue into one. That
stage strips everything already settled, mechanically implied, mechanical,
future or build-only, parks whatever waits on another choice, and admits only
genuinely open user-facing choices. Nothing is stripped or parked on a model's
say-so: every classification that takes an issue off the Ness-decision path must
carry a witness this controller re-proves from real settled source, anchored on
identity it held BEFORE the reply making the claim -- a question it composed, or
a signal an earlier audit routed against a real candidate. A name the reply
chose is not identity, and it does not become identity by being stored one run
earlier, so an issue this controller holds nothing for has no standing to reduce
and belongs in the check only as a genuine Ness choice. The controller then
independently refuses any admitted question whose composed form names
implementation machinery: mechanics belong to Claude and to this controller, and
only the consequence may ever reach Ness.

No raw model prose is ever surfaced. Every Ness-facing form is CONSTRUCTED BY
THIS CONTROLLER from fixed labels filled with bounded, single-line, validated
values, and is re-derived and compared byte-for-byte before it may be shown.
next-question-group delivers ONE coherent group at a time, recomputed from the
current preserved state on every call rather than from any pre-generated
questionnaire, so an answer can settle, remove, park or unpark later questions
before they are asked. Group size is presentation only: whatever does not fit
stays pending, nothing is ever dropped to keep a group short, and there is no
cap on how many questions the interview may ask.

record-ness-answer preserves Ness's exact words verbatim with their own digest,
and keeps three things permanently separate: what Ness said, how the controller
read it, and what mechanically follows. The reading is a deterministic match
against options the validated question itself declared -- never prose
interpretation -- and an answer that does not map to exactly one of them routes
to clarification rather than to a guess, with the exact words preserved either
way. Everything lives in one append-only, hash-chained JSONL journal outside
both Git repositories; the journal is the history and the standing is replayed
from it, so no answer is ever overwritten, a later explicit answer supersedes
rather than replaces, and a truncated line, broken hash link, sequence gap,
unknown event type or inconsistent replay fails closed instead of producing a
question.

A COMPLETE INVENTORY IS NOT CLEARANCE. One question-validation pass saying its
own check is complete is one model's account of its own completeness, so it no
longer unlocks anything by itself. The sequence is question-validation ->
complete package-bounded inventory -> FRESH INDEPENDENT question-coverage-review
-> and only then may interview-gate unlock. That review is a fresh, read-only,
ephemeral challenge to exactly that inventory -- never a second questionnaire
generator -- looking for a user-facing choice the first pass omitted, two
materially different N.H behaviours hidden under "mechanical", a policy
consequence behind technical wording, an older answer that does not settle the
new case, a premature question, or a supposed open question source already
settles. Finding nothing records one clearance bound to the exact package scope,
validation set, source binding, proved manifest, branch and HEAD; finding
something records routing signals instead and unlocks nothing. The clearance is
matched rather than remembered: a new validation, a new routed or review signal,
a new Ness answer, or any source drift withdraws it.

AND ADMITTING A QUESTION IS NOT DELIVERING IT. That same fresh review now stands
between an admitted question and Ness, not only between a complete inventory and
Claude, and it challenges the inventory in BOTH directions: a genuine user-facing
choice the first pass MISSED, and an open mechanical or configuration detail the
first pass turned into a user-facing choice and admitted as an OVER-ASK. Until a
current, matching clearance exists for its own package, a question
question-validation admitted is internally admitted and PROVISIONAL -- it is
counted, it blocks, next-question-group refuses to present it, and
deliverable_question_count is 0. A direction-B over-ask is still only a signal:
it binds to the standing target of the question it challenges, so one matter
keeps one held concern, and only question-validation may ever take an issue off
the Ness path, with the ordinary source-backed standing witness.

interview-gate then proves nothing genuine is still blocking AND that a current
fresh coverage clearance exists, and hands straight back to the unchanged
mechanical commands: remaining mechanical work is never a reason to ask Ness
anything, and a settled interview is still not acceptance, adoption,
PACKAGE_COMPLETE or Master/Map integration.

Commands:
    python3 nh_loop.py status
    python3 nh_loop.py preflight
    python3 nh_loop.py snapshot
    python3 nh_loop.py codex-smoke
    python3 nh_loop.py claude-smoke
    python3 nh_loop.py claude-read-smoke
    python3 nh_loop.py next-package
    python3 nh_loop.py prepare-next-claude-task
    python3 nh_loop.py execute-next-claude-task
    python3 nh_loop.py question-validation
    python3 nh_loop.py question-coverage-review
    python3 nh_loop.py interview-status
    python3 nh_loop.py next-question-group
    python3 nh_loop.py record-ness-answer      (JSON on stdin)
    python3 nh_loop.py route-audit-finding     (JSON on stdin)
    python3 nh_loop.py interview-gate

Authority order (informational only in v1 -- contents are not interpreted):
    1. NH_MASTER-20_CORRECTED_v10.md
    2. NH_DECISION_DEFAULTS-S19_v2_3.md
    3. current cursorrules
    4. NH_PROJECT_COMPANION_GOVERNANCE_AND_ARCHIVE_v1.md
    5. current Design and Wiring Map
"""

